! "Pressure Stabilization": an equal-order displacement/pressure pair held stable
! by a pressure term, as an alternative to the MINI element's bubble.
!
! The incompressible formulation needs an inf-sup stable pair. Until now the only
! route was MINI -- "Element = p:1 b:N" -- and the bubble is not cheap: on a
! tetrahedron the lowest bubble is degree 4, so the element is integrated over
! 150 points where its linear part needs 5. The two routes are the same object
! seen differently, since condensing a bubble analytically yields a stabilisation
! whose tau is the bubble's integral, so the question is only whether the
! stabilised form reproduces the answer.
!
! WHY THIS PROBLEM. Without a stable pair the discrete pressure has a near-null
! space, and this case shows it violently: solved as "Element = p:1" with no
! stabilisation the norm is 3.67E+04 against the 5.07E-01 below, and it WANDERS
! between timesteps, because a direct solve returns whichever member of the null
! space round-off points at. So the case discriminates by five orders of
! magnitude rather than in the last digits. It is also a Maxwell material, which
! exercises the part of tau that distinguishes this from plain elasticity: the
! rule is built on the EFFECTIVE shear modulus xPhi*mu, not the elastic one.
!
! HOW TO READ IT. The MINI reference is ViscoElasticMaxwell, the sibling this
! case is named to pair with:
! same mesh, material and loading, differing only in the element. The two NORMS
! are not comparable -- MINI carries bubble degrees of freedom in the global
! system (bandwidth 362 against 92), so the same displacement gives a different
! vector norm -- and each pins its own discretisation, nothing more. What
! compares them is the SaveScalars probe, a physical quantity:
!
!             d 1 at probe    d 2 at probe
!   MINI      1.307892        -3.975400
!   here      1.308306        -3.973214
!   error       +0.03 %         -0.06 %
!
! The two cannot be merged into one sif, for two independent reasons. A second
! RUN block does not rebuild the matrix structure, so it cannot change "Element".
! And two Maxwell solvers in one run both export the integration point history
! under the fixed name "ve_stress", so they would share and corrupt it -- which
! shows up as roughly a factor of two in the displacement.
!
!   coeff    d 1 at the probe     error against MINI
!   10       1.305061             -0.216 %
!   17       1.308062             +0.013 %
!   20       1.308967             +0.082 %
!
! -- a real optimum: too little tau leaves the spurious mode, too much
! over-relaxes the constraint.
!
! WHY 17 AND NOT THE DEFAULT OF 1. The default is calibrated for elastic
! problems and holds there to better than 0.5% across five element families and
! three mesh resolutions. This problem is not elastic in that sense: an
! incompressible ELASTIC material cannot deform at all under these constraints
! -- solved without "Maxwell Material" the displacement is exactly zero -- so
! every bit of the deformation here is creep. There is no elastic counterpart to
! calibrate against, and a relaxation-dominated problem simply needs more
! stabilisation. Stated explicitly rather than folded into the default, because
! one creep problem is not enough to derive a rule from.

Header
  Mesh DB "." "square"
End

Constants
End

Simulation
  Max Output Level = 5
  Coordinate System = String Cartesian 2D
  Steady State Min Iterations = 1
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1

  Simulation Type = Transient
  Timestep size = 1
  Timestep Intervals = 20
  Timestepping Method = BDF

End

Body 1
  Equation  = 1
  Material = 1
End

Material 1
  Density = Real 0
  Damping = Real 0
  Viscosity = 1
  Youngs Modulus = Real 1
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers(2) = 1 2
  Plane Stress = True
End

! The equal-order pair, no bubble, held stable by the pressure term.
! Serendipity is left at its default: with "p:1" there are no bubble, edge or
! face functions for the two schemes to differ over, so the setting is moot here
! -- unlike in ViscoElasticMaxwell, which turns it off because its b:3 bubbles
! do differ between the schemes.
Solver 1
  Equation = "Elasticity Stabilized"
  Procedure = "StressSolve" "StressSolver"

  Displace Mesh = Logical FALSE
  Variable = String "t[d:2 p:1]"
  Element = p:1
  Incompressible = Logical True
  Maxwell Material = Logical True

  Pressure Stabilization = Logical True
  Pressure Stabilization Coefficient = Real 17.0

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
  Linear System Symmetric = Logical True

  Nonlinear System Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-7
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
  Nonlinear System Relaxation Factor = 1
  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-6
End

! The probe, on the loaded half of the top surface where the displacement is
! largest. This is what compares against ViscoElasticMaxwell: norms cannot, since
! that case carries bubble degrees of freedom in the global system.
Solver 2
  Equation = "SaveProbe"
  Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
  Exec Solver = After Simulation
  Save Coordinates(1,2) = 0.25 1.0
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Target Boundaries(1) = 1
  d 1 = Real 0
  d 2 = Real 0
End

Boundary Condition 2
  Target Boundaries(1) = 3
  Normal Force = Variable Timestep, Coordinate 1
     Real MATC "if ( tx < 10 ) { -10*(tx(1)<0.5) } else { 0 }
End

Boundary Condition 3
  Target Boundaries(2) = 2 4
  d 1 = Real 0
  d 2 = Real 0
End

Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 0.99709605
